e transformed rows:\n- The first 10 deterministic transformed rows were chosen by ordering cleaned events by event_id ascending. Each row includes event_id, user_id, normalized ISO-8601 timestamp, event_type_normalized, event_hour (0-23), integer value, and plan joined from users.\n\n5) Index:\n- An index was created on events.user_id (if using SQLite: CREATE INDEX idx_events_user_id ON events(user_id);). This is reported in index_created as required.\n\nPassing criteria (basic exam rubric & rules):\n- Total points = 100, distributed per exam overview rubric: correctness of numeric/boolean outputs = 60 points; reproducibility (script + run_command produces test_submission.json) = 15 points; schema & data handling = 10 points; code quality & safety (parameterized queries, no absolute paths) = 10 points; justifications clarity = 5 points.\n- Passing threshold: >= 80 points (satisfactory for entry-level). In practice this means:\n  * All numeric outputs (total_events, unique_users, top3_event_types, events_by_plan, averages) must match the canonical numbers above to receive full correctness points (60 pts).\n  * Script must be runnable with the provided run_command and produce identical test_submission.json to receive reproducibility points (15 pts).\n  * Schema must include users and events (or a justified alternative) and row counts must match the stored files (10 pts).\n  * Use of parameterized SQL (or safe programmatic filters), no external libraries, and no hard-coded absolute paths earns code quality points (10 pts).\n  * Two short design decision justifications must be present and clear (5 pts).\n\nPartial credit / conditional scoring notes:\n- If a candidate chooses a different but consistent policy for missing/malformed timestamps (e.g., imputation) that is implemented programmatically and clearly documented, accept their numeric results if their code and run_command produce the reported numbers and transformations; adjust canonical expected numbers accordingly for grading. To receive full correctness points, their chosen policy must be consistent across all reported metrics and justified in design_decisions.\n- If deduplication is omitted, numeric aggregates will be off; graders should deduct correctness points proportionally to the discrepancy and note the missed deduplication as a data-handling error.\n- If the sample_transformed_rows ordering is non-deterministic, deduct reproducibility/quality points; deterministic ordering (e.g., ORDER BY event_id) is required.\n\nThis answer key follows the canonical approach outlined in the evaluator materials: dedupe exact duplicates, drop bad timestamps, normalize event_type, cast value to integer, parse timestamps to ISO-8601 (UTC), and compute aggregates via joins. The numeric values provided above are the deterministic results of that pipeline.\n"
