  "optional_attachments": "EXPLANATION BY FIELD:\n\nsubmission_metadata: Provided a model candidate name and timestamps matching a 90-minute attempt. time_taken_minutes = 90.\n\nassumptions: These document the key interpretative decisions required by the brief: (1) how to interpret the 8 full working days automation requirement given Charlie is 50% available, (2) defect turnaround expectations, (3) parallelization of testers, (4) test data prep, (5) security scan scheduling, (6) working-day conventions (exclude weekends and 2025-10-23). The automation assumption is critical and the evaluator should accept either interpretation if documented; this key chooses to do partial automation to keep schedule feasible.\n\nresources: Names and availability copied exactly from the brief (Alice 100, Bob 100, Charlie 50).\n\nschedule: The schedule was built to strictly respect the constraints in the brief:\n- Code freeze 2025-10-20: no testing depends on pre-freeze code.\n- Test Environment A usage is only scheduled between 2025-10-21 and 2025-10-30 (security scan 10-24; system testing 10-27..10-30).\n- UAT tasks scheduled between 2025-10-27 and 2025-10-31 (UAT environment available 10-27..11-01; we avoid weekend days and 11-01 is a Saturday so we use 10-31 as last working day in the window).\n- Public holiday 2025-10-23 omitted from working-day calculations (design duration 3 days between 10-21 and 10-24 counts Tue/Wed/Fri only).\n- Automation setup (targeted) scheduled for Charlie on 10-27..10-29; because Charlie is 50% (4h/day), 3 working days * 4h/day = 12 hours which matches the automation_setup estimate for F2.\n- Dates and duration_days are checked to be inclusive working days (durations equal number of weekdays in range excluding holiday/weekend).\n\ntest_scope: Each feature (F1..F6) uses exact IDs and complexity codes (L/M/H). Required test types reflect the brief and common sense: F2 and F5 include acceptance and higher priority test types; F6 includes perf test type. Priorities: F2 and F5 set to 1 (highest). F1 2, F3 & F4 3, F6 4.\n\nestimates: The estimates total 160 hours across all activities. Rationale and checks:\n- Test design total ~40h (fits into design window: 3 working days * 2 testers * 8h/day = 48 tester-hours available for design tasks).\n- Execution/regression totals fit into the system/UAT/regression windows where testers have capacity. System (4 days) + UAT (5 days) + regression (2 days) across two testers provide more than enough wall-clock tester hours to execute the estimated 84 test_execution hours when parallelized.\n- Automation_setup for F2 = 12 hours; at Charlie 4h/day -> 3 working days; scheduled 10-27..10-29. This is consistent with the assumption of partial automation.\n- The estimates omit automation_setup for other features since full 64h automation is infeasible within the window; that choice is defended in assumptions and contingency.\n\nrisk_prioritisation: Ranks map to feature complexity, explicit brief risks (Third-party API instability), and likely release impact. Each rationale is 1-2 sentences.\n\ncontingency_options: Two practical options were provided: (C1) reduce scope on lower-priority features, and (C2) postpone remaining automation. Both include expected quality impact and rationale. These are realistic given the tight window and Charlie's limited availability.\n\nentry_exit_criteria: Provided measurable criteria for each phase, including explicit conditions like 'No open critical defects', 'Regression pass rate >= 90%', 'Security scan completed before UAT'. Also included who signs off (QA lead/stakeholder) inside the strategy_summary for release day.\n\nstrategy_summary: Concise (well under 500 words). It ties schedule, risk and priorities and includes a brief communication plan for release day (who is notified when). The communication paragraph names Alice (QA lead), Release Manager, Product Owner and Dev Lead and gives notification timing (09:00 and post-deploy +1h/+2h updates).\n\nVALIDATION / CONSISTENCY NOTES:\n- All schedule assigned resources are one of: Alice, Bob, Charlie.\n- Dates are YYYY-MM-DD and chosen to be weekdays (no weekend dates are used as task start/end except where environment window included weekend but we used last working day instead).\n- The security scan is scheduled in Test Environment A (10-24) and completes before UAT start (10-27).\n- The schedule meets the fixed release date constraint: release sign-off and deployment scheduled 2025-11-03 (on or before the deadline).\n- Working day counting: durations match inclusive working days excluding the public holiday 2025-10-23 and weekends.\n\nPASSING CRITERIA (for evaluator):\nThe exam rubric in the evaluator materials defines a 100-point scoring. For a passing (basic level) solution the candidate should score >= 80 points. Below are concrete pass/fail checks derived from that rubric which an automated or manual grader can apply:\n\n1) Assumptions (>=3 sensible assumptions) — 5 points total\n- Award full 5 points if candidate lists at least 3 clear, relevant assumptions including an explicit statement about the automation 8-day interpretation or defect turnaround. Partial credit 1-4 points for fewer or weaker assumptions.\n\n2) Schedule realism & completeness — 30 points total\n- 20 points: Schedule respects dates, environment windows, public holiday and release date; contains dependencies and assigns resources. (Full 20 if all respected; 0 if blatant violation e.g., UAT scheduled outside window.)\n- 10 points: Logical sequencing and inclusion of milestones (code freeze, security scan, UAT start, release). (Full 10 if milestones present and correctly ordered.)\n\n3) Estimates — 15 points total\n- 10 points: Reasonable effort estimates aligned to complexity and resource capacity (i.e., totals do not exceed available tester hours grossly). Full credit if total estimated hours are consistent with schedule resource capacity given availability_percent.\n- 5 points: Aggregation